  "body": "https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#sniffing-a-mislabeled-binary-resource\nIs a section. And it defines what it calls \"rules for distinguishing if a resource is text or binary\". That sounds sensible ... until I get to the bits that say \"the computed MIME type is ...\" and my brain is all \"TYPE ERROR!\"\n\nOr at least, it would have done that if it hadn't already gone off on this this warning text at the end of the section (which, being red, bold, italic text on my screen, is quite eye-catching indeed):\n\n> It is critical that the rules for distinguishing if a resource is text or binary never determine the computed MIME type to be a scriptable MIME type, as this could allow a privilege escalation attack.\n\nI mean, really, doesn't the name sound like it should just be returning either \"text\" or \"binary\"?\n\nI don't know how feasible it would be to change the name (would be the smallest change here) or the \"return type\" (since apparently this algorithm doesn't technically return a value?), but maybe you could at least add some kind of a note at the beginning about the mismatch?\n",
